+        # Increase functional loss coefficient
+        lambda_F_content += 1e-3
 ```
 
+**Parameters with Typical Values:**
+*   **Optimizer:** Adam with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-4}$ [S1].
+*   **Loss Coefficients:** $\lambda_R = \lambda_P = \lambda_F = \lambda_D = 1$ [S1].
+*   **Disentanglement Weights:** $w_{sh} = w_{te} = w_{il} = 1 \times 10^{-3}$ and $w_{ex} = 1.0$ [S1].
+*   **Functional Loss Coefficient:** $\lambda_{F_{content}}$ starts at $1 \times 10^{-3}$ and increases by $1 \times 10^{-3}$ after each epoch [S1].
+*   **Input Resolution:** Face images resized to $512 \times 512$ pixels [S1].
+*   **Feature Map Dimensions:** $64 \times 64 \times 258$ [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Masking:** The method relies on accurate segmentation masks ($M_{wf}, M_f, M_e$) to ensure each stream generates features corresponding to the correct regions. Incorrect masks can lead to blending artifacts or failure to disentangle eyes from face [S1].
+*   **Rotation Matrix:** The rotation matrix $R$ must be correctly computed from the gaze label $g$ (pitch and yaw in radians) to ensure 3D consistency [S1].
+*   **Latent Code Initialization:** Latent codes are initialized from 3DMM parameters. Poor initialization may affect convergence or identity preservation [S1].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training involves optimizing two MLPs and latent codes via Adam optimizer [S1]. The explicit 3D rotation and feature compositing add minimal overhead to the standard NeRF volume rendering pipeline [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing two sets of MLP parameters and four latent codes per subject [S1]. Feature maps are low-resolution ($64 \times 64 \times 258$) [S1].
-*   **Performance:** On the ETH-XGaze dataset, GazeNeRF achieves superior gaze redirection accuracy compared to STED and HeadNeRF, while maintaining similar image quality metrics (SSIM, PSNR, LPIPS) to HeadNeRF [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Explicit 3D awareness of eyeball rotation; disentangled features prevent identity loss and blurry eyes; high-fidelity rendering [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires face parsing masks for training; relies on 3DMM initialization for latent codes; 2D gaze label input limits head pose control compared to full 3D head pose conditioning [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When 3D-consistent gaze redirection is required, especially for applications needing accurate eye rotation without distorting the rest of the face [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity relighting is the primary goal (EyeNeRF is better suited) or when 2D-only processing is sufficient and 3D awareness is not critical [S1].
